ruz</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><name><surname>Bergman</surname><given-names>RN</given-names></name><name><surname>Goran</surname><given-names>MI</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Unique effect of visceral fat on insulin sensitivity in obese Hispanic children with a family history of type 2 diabetes</article-title><source>Diabetes Care</source><year>2002</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>1631</fpage><lpage>1636</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12196439</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B20"><label>20</label><element-citation publication-type="book"><source>The IDF consensus worldwide definition of the metabolic syndrome</source><date-in-citation>accessed July 18, 2006</date-in-citation><publisher-name>International Diabetes Federation</publisher-name><comment>Available from: URL: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.idf.org/webdata/docs/Metabolic_syndrome_definition.pdf">http://www.idf.org/webdata/docs/Metabolic_syndrome_definition.pdf</ext-link></comment></element-citation></ref><ref id="B21"><label>21</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><collab>Expert Committee on the Diagnosis and Classification of Diabetes Mellitus</collab><article-title>American Diabetes Association: clinical practice recommendations 2002</article-title><source>Diabetes Care</source><year>2002</year><volume>25</volume><issue>Suppl 1</issue><fpage>S1</fpage><lpage>S147</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11788484</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B22"><label>22</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wei</surname><given-names>JN</given-names></name><name><surname>Sung</surname><given-names>FC</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>CC</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Chiang</surname><given-names>CC</given-names></name><name><surname>Chuang</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>National surveillance for type 2 diabetes mellitus in Taiwanese children</article-title><source>JAMA</source><year>2003</year><volume>290</volume><fpage>1345</fpage><lpage>1350</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12966126</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Agreement rate of the prevalence of the MS in children and adolescents using the three sets of criteria. MS, metabolic syndrome.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-563-g001"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Criteria Used to Identify the Metabolic Syndrome in Children and Adolescents in This Study</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-563-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>NCEP-ATP III, National Cholesterol Education Program--Adult Treatment Panel III; HDL, high-density lipoprotein; OGTT, oral glucose tolerance test.</p><p><sup>*</sup>A cutoff point for high blood pressure using the sex-, age-, and height-specific 90th percentile, while this study used a sex- and age-specific 90th percentile cutoff only because of the small number of cases.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>General Characteristics of the Study Subjects by Gender</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-563-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>BMI, body mass index; DM, diabetes mellitus; CVA, cerebrovascular accident; CVD, cardiovascular disease.</p><p><sup>*</sup><italic>p</italic> value calculated using the &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> test.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Comparison of the Prevalence of the Metabolic Syndrome in Children and Adolescents for Each Set of Criteria</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-563-i003"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>HDL, high-density lipoprotein.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T4" position="float"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Comparison of the Prevalence of the Metabolic Syndrome according to the Degree of Obesity in the Different Sets of Criteria</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-563-i004"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>NA, not available.</p><p><sup>*</sup><italic>p</italic> value calculated using the &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> test.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T5" position="float"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>Agreement of the Prevalence of the Metabolic Syndrome in Children and Adolescents Using the Three Sets of Criteria</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-563-i005"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>Percentage of participants who were classified as either having or not having the MS using all three sets of criteria.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>